# 03. Responsive Philosophy

GrowFlow uses **responsive composition**, not merely responsive resizing.

The interface should:

- preserve hierarchy;
- preserve task meaning;
- preserve access to important actions;
- reduce unnecessary visual density on smaller screens;
- adapt navigation;
- adapt data presentation;
- adapt interaction mechanisms.

The interface must not simply shrink desktop UI until it becomes unusable.

---

# 04. Responsive Priority Order

When viewport space decreases, preserve information in this order:

```text
1. Current task/context
2. Primary action
3. Critical status
4. Essential data
5. Navigation access
6. Secondary metadata
7. Supporting information
8. Decorative content
```

Decorative content is removed before functional information.

---

# 05. Breakpoint Model

Canonical breakpoint model:

| Breakpoint | Width | Primary Behavior |
|---|---:|---|
| Mobile | <768px | Single-column, drawer navigation |
| Tablet | 768–1199px | Adaptive two-column/compressed layouts |
| Desktop | >=1200px | Full information-density layouts |

These breakpoints are behavioral defaults, not rigid requirements for every component.

Components may use container-based adaptation when that produces better behavior.

---

# 06. Container Responsiveness

Page content should use responsive containers rather than hard-coded viewport widths.

Conceptual model:

```text
Viewport
 ↓
Global shell
 ↓
Content container
 ↓
Responsive grid
 ↓
Feature content
```

Content should remain readable and avoid excessive line lengths.

---

# 07. Desktop Behavior

Desktop is the primary high-density workplace environment.

Desktop may expose:

- persistent sidebar;
- multi-column layouts;
- dense tables;
- dashboard grids;
- side-by-side panels;
- contextual detail panes.

Desktop does not justify unnecessary information overload.

---

# 08. Tablet Behavior

Tablet is an intermediate composition, not a scaled desktop.

Typical adaptations:

- sidebar may remain available but become narrower;
- multi-column grids reduce columns;
- secondary panels may stack;
- table columns reduce;
- filters move into drawers/popovers;
- large navigation groups may collapse.

---

# 09. Mobile Behavior

Mobile uses a focused single-column workflow.

Typical behavior:

```text
Top navigation
 ↓
Page context
 ↓
Primary content
 ↓
Primary action
 ↓
Secondary content
```

The sidebar becomes a drawer according to Phase 06E.

---

# 10. Mobile Navigation

Mobile navigation must preserve access to all authorized primary destinations.

The sidebar becomes a drawer.

Drawer requirements:

- accessible trigger;
- accessible name;
- focus management;
- escape behavior;
- backdrop behavior;
- focus restoration;
- scroll containment.

---

# 11. Mobile Top Navigation

The global top navigation remains global.

Mobile may collapse:

- public links;
- secondary actions;
- search;
- account actions.

However, the following concepts remain accessible:

- GrowFlow identity;
- Workplace;
- search;
- notifications;
- profile/authentication.

---

# 12. Workplace Selector on Mobile

The Workplace selector must remain discoverable.

If horizontal space is limited:

```text
TopNav
→ compact workplace control
→ full selector drawer/menu
```

The role/workplace meaning must remain explicit.

---

# 13. Sidebar Drawer Behavior

Opening:

```text
trigger
 ↓
drawer
 ↓
focus enters drawer
```

Closing:

```text
close / escape / navigation
 ↓
drawer closes
 ↓
focus returns appropriately
```

Navigation should close the drawer after successful route transition.

---

# 14. Mobile Search

Global search may open as:

- expanded top navigation search;
- dedicated search surface;
- full-width overlay.

Search semantics remain identical to desktop.

Search remains authorization-aware.

---

# 15. Mobile Notifications

Notification list should:

- remain readable;
- expose unread state;
- support canonical deep links;
- preserve timestamps/status;
- avoid overly dense metadata.

Notification preview may use a drawer.

Full notification content remains route-addressable.

---

# 16. Mobile Profile and Settings

Profile/settings actions should remain reachable without requiring desktop-only controls.

Account actions must not be hidden behind inaccessible hover behavior.

---

# 17. Responsive Layout Grid

Grid behavior should adapt by semantic content.

Example:

```text
Desktop:
4 → 3 → 2 → 1 columns

Tablet:
3 → 2 columns

Mobile:
1 column
```

Exact column counts depend on content.

Never compress cards below usable content width merely to preserve column count.

---

# 18. Responsive Spacing

Use the canonical spacing scale.

Smaller screens may reduce:

- section gaps;
- outer padding;
- inter-card spacing.

Do not remove spacing so aggressively that hierarchy collapses.

---

# 19. Responsive Typography

Typography remains based on the canonical type scale.

At smaller sizes:

- display headings may step down;
- body readability remains prioritized;
- metadata must remain legible;
- line lengths must remain manageable.

Do not shrink body text excessively to fit more content.

---

# 20. Minimum Text Size Principle

Body and interactive text must remain readable.

Responsive adaptation should reduce:

- width;
- density;
- secondary metadata;

before reducing essential text to impractical sizes.

---

# 21. Touch Targets

Interactive controls should generally provide approximately:

```text
44 × 44px
```

of usable touch target area where practical.

Icons may be visually smaller while their interactive hit area remains accessible.

---

# 22. Touch Spacing

Adjacent interactive targets must have sufficient separation to prevent accidental activation.

Do not place tiny icon buttons directly against each other without adequate hit areas.

---

# 23. Hover Independence

No essential interaction may depend solely on hover.

Every hover-revealed function must have an equivalent:

- click;
- keyboard;
- touch;
- or always-visible action.

---

# 24. Pointer Adaptation

Interfaces should account for:

```text
mouse
touch
keyboard
stylus
assistive technology
```

Interaction must not depend on a specific pointing device.

---

# 25. Responsive Tables

Tables are high-risk responsive components.

Do not simply force a wide desktop table into a narrow viewport.

Use an appropriate strategy:

```text
Desktop
→ full table

Tablet
→ reduced columns / horizontal scroll

Mobile
→ priority columns / stacked rows / list representation
```

The semantic data must remain accessible.

---

# 26. Table Horizontal Scrolling

If horizontal scrolling is necessary:

- indicate scrollability where useful;
- preserve header/data alignment;
- avoid clipping critical information;
- keep row actions accessible.

Do not hide critical fields merely because the viewport is narrow.

---

# 27. Table-to-List Transformation

A table may transform into a structured list/card representation on mobile.

The transformed representation must preserve:

- identity;
- status;
- primary metadata;
- actions;
- sorting/filtering semantics;
- accessibility.

This is a representation change, not a domain-model change.

---

# 28. Dense Admin Tables

Admin tables may remain horizontally scrollable longer than public tables because governance workflows require dense information.

However:

- critical actions remain accessible;
- row identity remains visible;
- keyboard navigation remains usable.

---

# 29. Mentor Tables

Mentor student/project tables should prioritize:

1. student/project identity;
2. health;
3. lifecycle/status;
4. important action.

Secondary metadata can collapse.

---

# 30. Student Task Tables

Student task interfaces should prioritize:

1. task;
2. status;
3. milestone/context;
4. due date;
5. actionable controls.

Secondary metadata may move into detail views.

---

# 31. Responsive Dashboards

Dashboards must transform composition rather than simply shrink widgets.

Desktop:

```text
multiple metric/data regions
```

Tablet:

```text
fewer columns
```

Mobile:

```text
vertical priority order
```

Critical metrics appear before supporting visualizations.

---

# 32. Dashboard Widget Order

Responsive order should follow user decision priority, not DOM convenience.

Example:

```text
Project health
→ current progress
→ urgent risks
→ next actions
→ supporting activity
```

---

# 33. Partial Failure on Responsive Dashboards

A failed widget must retain its allocated hierarchy.

The error state should fit naturally within the responsive layout.

---

# 34. Charts on Mobile

Charts should:

- remain readable;
- avoid microscopic labels;
- support alternate data representations;
- allow horizontal interaction only when necessary;
- preserve accessible summaries.

If a chart cannot remain meaningful, provide a data table/list alternative.

---

# 35. Data Visualization Accessibility

Charts must not communicate meaning through color alone.

Use:

- labels;
- patterns where appropriate;
- icons;
- textual summaries;
- legends;
- accessible data tables.

---

# 36. Responsive Forms

Forms should move from multi-column desktop composition to single-column mobile composition where appropriate.

Preferred:

```text
Desktop:
Field A | Field B

Mobile:
Field A
Field B
```

Do not create excessively narrow input fields.

---

# 37. Form Action Placement

Primary form actions must remain easy to reach.

On mobile, long forms may use:

- sticky action areas;
- bottom action bars;
- clearly separated action sections.

Sticky actions must not cover form fields.

---

# 38. Form Error Behavior

Errors must remain adjacent to their associated fields.

On mobile:

- do not rely only on red borders;
- preserve readable error text;
- move focus appropriately after failed submission when useful.

---

# 39. Keyboard Accessibility

Every interactive capability must be reachable by keyboard unless the interaction inherently requires a non-keyboard physical action.

Required support includes:

- Tab;
- Shift+Tab;
- Enter;
- Space;
- Escape;
- arrow keys where component patterns require them.

---

# 40. Focus Visibility

Focus indicators must be:

- visible;
- sufficiently contrasted;
- consistent;
- not removed by CSS resets.

Do not use:

```css
outline: none;
```

without a valid replacement.

---

# 41. Focus Order

Focus order must follow logical reading and interaction order.

Avoid CSS positioning that causes visual order to diverge substantially from DOM order.

---

# 42. Skip Navigation

Authenticated and public shells must provide a skip mechanism to the main content region.

Conceptual:

```text
Skip to main content
```

It should become visible when focused.

---

# 43. Landmark Structure

Pages should use semantic landmarks where appropriate:

```text
header
nav
main
aside
footer
```

There should be one clear primary `main` region.

---

# 44. Heading Hierarchy

Headings must follow logical hierarchy.

Avoid:

```text
H1
→ H4
```

merely because the visual style looks appropriate.

Visual appearance must be controlled by tokens, not incorrect semantic heading levels.

---

# 45. Accessible Names

Interactive controls must have accessible names.

Icon-only controls require explicit accessible labels.

Examples:

```text
Open navigation
Search
Notifications
Close
More actions
Share
```

---

# 46. Icon Semantics

Decorative icons should be hidden from assistive technology when they convey no independent meaning.

Meaningful icons require appropriate labels or accompanying text.

---

# 47. Button vs Link

Use:

```text
Link → navigation
Button → action
```

Do not use clickable `div` elements as substitutes.

---

# 48. Native HTML First

Prefer semantic/native controls before building custom interaction patterns.

Examples:

- `button`;
- `input`;
- `select` where appropriate;
- `textarea`;
- `a`;
- `form`.

Custom controls must reproduce equivalent keyboard and assistive technology behavior.

---

# 49. Dialog Accessibility

Dialogs must:

- have accessible names;
- identify descriptions where useful;
- trap focus appropriately;
- support Escape where appropriate;
- restore focus;
- prevent background interaction when modal.

---

# 50. Drawer Accessibility

Drawers must have:

- accessible name;
- focus management;
- keyboard dismissal;
- background interaction control where modal;
- logical focus order.

---

# 51. Popover Accessibility

Popover behavior must be predictable.

Trigger should communicate:

```text
closed/open
```

where required.

Focus behavior depends on whether the popover is:

- menu;
- tooltip;
- dialog;
- non-modal informational surface.

Do not treat all popovers identically.

---

# 52. Menu Accessibility

Menus should use appropriate keyboard behavior:

```text
Arrow Up/Down
Enter
Escape
```

Focus must remain coherent.

---

# 53. Tabs Accessibility

Tabs must expose:

- selected state;
- associated panel;
- keyboard navigation;
- focus;
- orientation where applicable.

Do not implement tabs as unrelated links if they are semantically tab controls.

---

# 54. Accordion Accessibility

Accordions must expose:

- expanded/collapsed state;
- associated content;
- keyboard operation.

---

# 55. Tooltips

Tooltips are supplementary.

Never put essential instructions or actions only inside a tooltip.

Touch devices require an equivalent accessible mechanism.

---

# 56. Toast Accessibility

Toasts should use appropriate live-region behavior.

Avoid announcing every minor UI event.

Critical failures and meaningful completion events may require stronger announcement.

---

# 57. Live Regions

Live regions should be used selectively.

Appropriate:

- AI completion;
- critical background operation failure;
- important connection state;
- form submission result.

Inappropriate:

- every SSE event;
- every progress percentage;
- every typing interaction.

---

# 58. Screen Reader Navigation

Screen-reader users should be able to understand:

- page title/context;
- current section;
- current state;
- available actions;
- status changes;
- errors;
- navigation.

Visual grouping must have meaningful semantic equivalents.

---

# 59. Form Labels

Every form control must have a programmatically associated label.

Placeholder text is not a label.

---

# 60. Required Fields

Required status must be communicated semantically and visually.

Do not rely solely on an asterisk without accessible context.

---

# 61. Form Error Association

Error messages should be programmatically associated with the relevant control.

The user should be able to determine:

```text
which field failed
+
why
+
how to fix it
```

---

# 62. Validation Timing

Validation should avoid being excessively aggressive.

Preferred:

- immediate validation for obvious constraints;
- field-level feedback after interaction;
- comprehensive feedback on submit.

---

# 63. Password and Authentication Forms

Authentication forms must provide:

- labels;
- accessible errors;
- password visibility control if provided;
- keyboard submission;
- clear focus;
- loading state;
- server error state.

---

# 64. Authentication Responsive Behavior

Login/register pages may use distinct compositions while retaining the unified design system.

Mobile should prioritize:

```text
brand
→ context
→ form
→ primary action
→ recovery/alternative action
```

Decorative imagery must not interfere with authentication.

---

# 65. Public Hero Accessibility

Hero visual storytelling must not make the primary statement inaccessible.

Ensure:

- semantic heading;
- meaningful CTA;
- image alternative text where meaningful;
- decorative imagery hidden appropriately;
- keyboard-accessible interactions.

---

# 66. Editorial Image Accessibility

Contextual/editorial images must be classified:

```text
informative
decorative
functional
```

Informative images need meaningful alternatives.

Decorative images should not create redundant screen-reader noise.

---

# 67. Background Images

Background imagery must not carry essential information unless an equivalent textual representation exists.

---

# 68. Animation Accessibility

Animations must communicate no essential meaning exclusively through motion.

Every animated state needs a static equivalent.

---

# 69. Reduced Motion

Respect:

```text
prefers-reduced-motion
```

When enabled:

- reduce or remove decorative movement;
- shorten transitions;
- disable parallax;
- remove floating motion;
- avoid continuous animated backgrounds.

---

# 70. GrowFlow Float → Settle Under Reduced Motion

The canonical Float → Settle interaction becomes:

```text
subtle fade / immediate settle
```

when reduced motion is enabled.

The interaction remains understandable.

---

# 71. AI Animation Under Reduced Motion

AI interfaces must not rely on:

- pulsing neon;
- fake typing;
- continuous glow;
- animated particles.

Reduced-motion mode should present clear textual status instead.

---

# 72. Loading Accessibility

Skeletons must not be the only indication that content is loading.

Where useful:

```text
Loading projects
```

should be exposed semantically.

---

# 73. Spinner Accessibility

Spinners should not generate excessive screen-reader announcements.

Use an appropriate loading label at the relevant container level.

---

# 74. Progress Indicators

Determinate progress should expose actual progress.

Indeterminate progress should not imply a percentage.

Never fabricate:

```text
73%
```

when the backend does not provide truthful progress.

---

# 75. AI Progress Accessibility

Expose meaningful stages:

```text
Generating
Validating
Quality checking
Saving
Completed
```

rather than internal chain-of-thought.

---

# 76. Background Job Accessibility

Long-running jobs should expose:

- current state;
- completion/failure;
- cancellation availability;
- retry availability.

Users should not have to visually watch a progress animation.

---

# 77. Status Semantics

Status should be communicated through:

- text;
- semantic attributes where appropriate;
- icons as supporting cues;
- color as supplementary information.

---

# 78. Color Independence

Never communicate:

```text
green = success
red = failure
```

without additional semantic information.

Use labels/icons/structure as appropriate.

---

# 79. Contrast

Text, controls, focus indicators, borders, and status indicators must meet applicable accessibility contrast requirements.

Muted does not mean unreadable.

---

# 80. Dark Mode Accessibility

Dark mode must preserve:

- text contrast;
- focus visibility;
- status differentiation;
- chart readability;
- form clarity.

Dark mode is not simply color inversion.

---

# 81. Zoom and Text Scaling

The layout must remain usable under browser zoom and increased text size.

Do not:

- clip text;
- hide controls;
- create overlapping content;
- make dialogs unusable.

---

# 82. Reflow

At high zoom, content should reflow into a readable single-column composition where practical.

Horizontal scrolling should be limited to content that inherently requires width, such as complex tables.

---

# 83. Text Expansion

UI components must tolerate longer text.

Avoid fixed widths that assume English labels will always fit.

---

# 84. Dynamic Content

Dynamic content insertion must not unexpectedly move focus or disorient the user.

Examples:

- notification arriving;
- task updating;
- AI stage changing.

The interface should update without stealing focus.

---

# 85. Focus During Navigation

After route transition, focus should move to an appropriate page heading/main region when needed.

This is especially important for screen-reader and keyboard users.

---

# 86. Focus During Error Recovery

If a page-level error occurs:

- move focus to the error heading when appropriate;
- provide retry;
- preserve navigation.

If a local widget fails, focus should remain near the affected feature.

---

# 87. Route Announcements

Route changes should expose the new page/context to assistive technologies.

Document titles and meaningful headings should update appropriately.

---

# 88. Modal Route Interaction

Opening a modal should not destroy the underlying page context.

Closing it should restore context.

---

# 89. Responsive Modals

Desktop:

```text
centered modal
```

Mobile:

```text
near-full-width modal
or
bottom-sheet-like composition
```

depending on content.

Semantics remain dialog-based.

---

# 90. Responsive Drawers

Drawers may become:

- side drawers on desktop/tablet;
- full-width or near-full-width surfaces on mobile.

They must remain keyboard accessible.

---

# 91. Sticky Elements

Sticky headers/action bars must:

- not cover focused controls;
- respect safe areas;
- remain readable;
- avoid excessive screen occupation.

---

# 92. Mobile Safe Areas

Where relevant, account for device safe areas around:

- top navigation;
- bottom action bars;
- drawers;
- full-screen dialogs.

---

# 93. Orientation Changes

The application should remain usable when users rotate devices.

Do not assume a single mobile orientation.

---

# 94. Keyboard on Mobile

Forms must remain usable when the virtual keyboard opens.

Avoid fixed bottom controls that obscure focused inputs.

---

# 95. Mobile Long Forms

Long forms should:

- preserve section hierarchy;
- support natural scrolling;
- maintain focus visibility;
- avoid trapping the user.

---

# 96. Mobile AI Workflows

AI generation screens should prioritize:

1. operation state;
2. current stage;
3. key actions;
4. result/review;
5. secondary execution detail.

Detailed traces remain accessible but should not dominate mobile.

---

# 97. Mobile Blueprint Review

Blueprint review must remain usable without requiring a wide desktop canvas.

Use:

- collapsible sections;
- stacked content;
- horizontal scrolling only for genuinely wide structures;
- clear section navigation.

---

# 98. Mobile Roadmap

Roadmaps/timelines should adapt.

Possible representation:

```text
Desktop → timeline/calendar
Mobile → vertical chronological list
```

The underlying milestone/date semantics remain identical.

---

# 99. Mobile Project Workspace

Primary navigation should prioritize:

```text
Overview
Blueprint
Tasks
Milestones
Risks
Roadmap
Documents
AI Mentor
```

Secondary features remain accessible through navigation rather than disappearing.

---

# 100. Mobile Mentor Workspace

Mentor mobile workflows should prioritize:

```text
Students
Projects
At Risk
Help Requests
Activity
```

Group/project detail remains reachable.

---

# 101. Mobile Admin Workspace

Admin mobile should prioritize:

```text
Overview
Critical monitoring
Investigations
Security/Audit
```

Dense observability tables may use horizontal scrolling or detail routes.

---

# 102. Responsive Breadcrumbs

Breadcrumbs may collapse on mobile.

At minimum, preserve:

- current context;
- meaningful parent navigation.

Avoid rendering an unreadably long breadcrumb chain.

---

# 103. Responsive Tabs

If tabs cannot fit:

- horizontal scroll;
- dropdown/select representation;
- wrapped layout where appropriate.

Do not truncate tab labels into ambiguity.

---

# 104. Responsive Filters

Filters may transform:

```text
Desktop → visible filter bar
Mobile → filter drawer
```

Active filter count should remain discoverable.

---

# 105. Responsive Sorting

Sorting controls must remain accessible.

On mobile, sorting may move into a menu.

---

# 106. Responsive Bulk Actions

Bulk action bars must not obscure content.

On mobile:

```text
selected count
→ action menu
```

may replace a wide desktop action toolbar.

---

# 107. Responsive Context Actions

Primary action remains prominent.

Secondary actions may move into:

```text
More actions
```

but must remain keyboard/touch accessible.

---

# 108. Responsive Detail Pages

Desktop may show:

```text
main content | contextual panel
```

Mobile should generally become:

```text
main content
↓
contextual panel
```

or an explicit drawer.

---

# 109. Responsive Split Views

Split views must not create unusably narrow panes.

At smaller widths, switch to a single-pane navigation model.

---

# 110. Document Viewer Responsiveness

Document viewer must support:

- readable text width;
- zoom;
- code wrapping/scrolling as appropriate;
- mobile navigation;
- version controls.

---

# 111. Code Blocks

Code blocks may horizontally scroll if wrapping would destroy code meaning.

Scrolling must remain accessible.

---

# 112. Markdown Responsiveness

Generated Markdown content should:

- wrap prose;
- preserve code;
- preserve tables through responsive strategies;
- avoid overflowing viewport.

---

# 113. Responsive Image Behavior

Images should:

- scale proportionally;
- avoid layout shift;
- preserve meaningful crop where intentional;
- remain accessible.

---

# 114. Responsive Video/Media

Media must not overflow its container.

Controls must remain accessible on touch and keyboard.

---

# 115. Accessible Links

Links must have understandable names in context.

Avoid repetitive:

```text
Read more
```

without enough surrounding context for assistive technology.

---

# 116. Accessible Data Tables

Tables must provide:

- meaningful headers;
- row/column relationships;
- captions where useful;
- status text;
- accessible action names.

---

# 117. Table Selection Accessibility

Checkbox selection should communicate:

- selected/unselected;
- row identity;
- bulk selection context.

---

# 118. Accessible Pagination

Pagination controls should communicate:

- current page;
- available pages/actions;
- disabled state;
- previous/next semantics.

---

# 119. Accessible Search

Search must expose:

- input label;
- result count where useful;
- loading;
- no results;
- errors.

Do not rely only on visual result updates.

---

# 120. Search Keyboard Behavior

Support:

- focus;
- typing;
- result navigation;
- Enter;
- Escape where applicable.

Keyboard shortcuts must never prevent normal typing behavior.

---

# 121. Notification Accessibility

Notifications should communicate:

- unread/read;
- source/context;
- time;
- actionability.

Live notifications should not constantly interrupt users.

---

# 122. Accessible Activity Feeds

Activity feeds should expose meaningful event descriptions rather than raw event IDs.

---

# 123. Accessible Status Chips

Status chips should contain textual state.

Color and icon are supplementary.

---

# 124. Accessible Health Indicators

Health indicators should communicate:

```text
Healthy
Degraded
At Risk
Unknown
```

through text, not color alone.

---

# 125. Accessible Risk Indicators

Risk severity should be explicitly labeled.

Example:

```text
High severity
```

not merely a red icon.

---

# 126. Accessible AI State

AI state should be understandable without visual animation.

Example:

```text
Blueprint generation — Running
Current stage — Validation
```

---

# 127. Accessible Errors

Errors should be:

- perceivable;
- associated;
- actionable;
- non-destructive to existing valid content.

---

# 128. Error Summary

For complex forms, an error summary may appear above the form.

Each error should link/focus to its field.

---

# 129. Network Error Accessibility

Connectivity state should be communicated textually.

Example:

```text
Connection lost. Reconnecting…
```

Do not rely only on an icon.

---

# 130. SSE Accessibility

SSE connection changes should not create constant announcements.

Only meaningful user-impacting state changes should be announced.

---

# 131. Permission Error Accessibility

403 states should clearly communicate that the action/resource is unavailable without exposing protected information.

---

# 132. Not Found Accessibility

404 states should provide:

- meaningful heading;
- explanation;
- safe navigation;
- retry where appropriate.

---

# 133. Suspended Account Accessibility

Account-state screens should clearly explain that interaction is unavailable and provide the appropriate next step.

---

# 134. Responsive Authentication Deep Links

Deep-link login flows must remain functional on mobile.

Return intent must survive the authentication flow where safe.

---

# 135. Responsive Share

Share interactions must work on:

- desktop clipboard;
- mobile share mechanisms where supported;
- fallback copy behavior.

---

# 136. Clipboard Accessibility

Copy controls need accessible names and confirmation.

---

# 137. Motion Governance

All motion must follow:

```text
Functional motion
→ allowed when useful

Decorative motion
→ subtle

Continuous motion
→ rare

Reduced motion
→ minimized/removed
```

---

# 138. Motion Duration

Use Phase 06C motion tokens.

Do not introduce arbitrary transition durations in individual components.

---

# 139. Motion Easing

Use canonical easing tokens.

Avoid custom per-component motion curves without justification.

---

# 140. Motion Performance

Animations should avoid:

- expensive layout thrashing;
- unnecessary repaint;
- large DOM animation;
- continuous CPU usage.

Prefer composited properties where appropriate.

---

# 141. Responsive Motion

Motion should be reduced on smaller devices when it would harm performance or usability.

Do not remove motion solely because the viewport is smaller; remove it when it becomes unnecessary or costly.

---

# 142. Accessibility and AI Streaming

AI streaming must not create:

- rapidly changing focus;
- screen-reader spam;
- inaccessible progress;
- hidden completion;
- visual-only status.

---

# 143. Accessibility and Background Jobs

Users should be able to leave an operation and return later without losing context.

Completion must be represented through:

- notification;
- status;
- activity;
- canonical resource state.

---

# 144. Responsive Long-Running Workflows

Long-running workflow pages must remain usable when:

- the user changes orientation;
- viewport changes;
- network reconnects;
- browser resumes;
- user navigates away.

Backend-owned execution remains the source of truth.

---

# 145. Device Capability Assumptions

Do not assume:

- large screen;
- mouse;
- hover;
- fast CPU;
- stable network;
- persistent tab;
- high bandwidth.

Core functionality must survive reasonable constrained environments.

---

# 146. Progressive Enhancement

Where practical:

```text
baseline semantic HTML
 ↓
enhanced interaction
 ↓
enhanced visual behavior
```

The interface should degrade gracefully rather than becoming unusable.

---

# 147. JavaScript Failure Considerations

Core navigation and important content should have sensible structural semantics even when enhanced behavior fails.

The exact level of no-JS support depends on framework/runtime architecture, but semantic structure remains mandatory.

---

# 148. Browser Zoom Testing

Test at representative zoom levels, including approximately:

```text
100%
125%
200%
```

and where relevant higher scaling.

Check:

- navigation;
- forms;
- dialogs;
- tables;
- dashboards;
- project workspace.

---

# 149. Text Resize Testing

Test increased text size independently where tooling supports it.

Check for:

- clipping;
- overlap;
- inaccessible actions;
- broken layouts.

---

# 150. Keyboard-Only Test Matrix

At minimum test:

```text
Tab
Shift+Tab
Enter
Space
Escape
Arrow keys
Home/End where applicable
```

through:

- public nav;
- auth;
- sidebar;
- search;
- notifications;
- forms;
- tables;
- dialogs;
- AI workflows.

---

# 151. Screen Reader Test Matrix

Test representative flows with supported screen readers.

Verify:

- page context;
- heading hierarchy;
- form labels;
- errors;
- dialog behavior;
- dynamic status;
- navigation;
- table semantics.

---

# 152. Responsive Test Matrix

At minimum:

```text
Small mobile
Large mobile
Tablet portrait
Tablet landscape
Desktop
Wide desktop
```

Exact viewport values belong in the test harness.

---

# 153. Component-Level Responsive Testing

Each reusable component should document whether it:

```text
scales
reflows
collapses
scrolls
transforms
hides secondary content
```

Do not leave responsive behavior to page-by-page improvisation.

---

# 154. Component Responsive Contract

A component contract should define:

```text
Desktop behavior
Tablet behavior
Mobile behavior
Keyboard behavior
Touch behavior
Reduced-motion behavior
Overflow behavior
```

where relevant.

---

# 155. Layout Overflow Policy

Unexpected horizontal overflow is a defect.

Intentional horizontal scrolling is allowed only for content that genuinely requires width.

---

# 156. Overflow Debugging

During development, inspect:

- fixed widths;
- absolute positioning;
- long strings;
- tables;
- code;
- generated content;
- third-party widgets.

---

# 157. Long Content Resilience

Test with:

- long project names;
- long task titles;
- long mentor names;
- long document titles;
- long AI-generated headings;
- long notification text.

UI must not collapse.

---

# 158. Empty Content Resilience

Responsive layouts must remain balanced when optional metadata is missing.

Do not assume every card/row contains all fields.

---

# 159. Dynamic Content Resilience

Backend may return:

- additional statuses;
- longer labels;
- additional metadata.

Components must fail gracefully.

---

# 160. Internationalization Readiness

Responsive layouts should tolerate future translation.

Avoid fixed-width labels and assumptions about English word length.

---

# 161. Accessible Number Formatting

Counts and metrics must remain understandable to assistive technologies.

Avoid exposing only abbreviations such as:

```text
1.2k
```

when the exact value is meaningful.

---

# 162. Accessible Dates

Dates should have semantic/readable representations.

Visual relative time may be supplemented with exact timestamps where useful.

---

# 163. Accessible Time

Duration and timestamps should be understandable without color or relative-position cues.

---

# 164. Responsive Calendar/Roadmap

Calendar interfaces must have an accessible alternate representation where visual grid interaction is difficult.

The chronological task/milestone data must remain available in a list representation.

---

# 165. Accessible Calendar/Roadmap

Users must be able to determine:

- date;
- milestone/task;
- status;
- selection;
- navigation.

Keyboard and screen-reader patterns must be explicitly designed.

---

# 166. Responsive Project Graphs/Diagrams

If future project visualizations use graph-like layouts, provide an accessible structured representation.

Do not make a canvas/diagram the only representation of project relationships.

---

# 167. Accessible Progress Visualization

Visual progress must have textual equivalent:

```text
Progress: 62%
```

when actual percentage exists.

---

# 168. Accessible Dashboard Summary

Complex dashboards should expose a meaningful summary before detailed visualizations.

---

# 169. Responsive AI Mentor Chat

Chat interface should adapt:

Desktop:

```text
conversation
+
context panel
```

Mobile:

```text
conversation
+
context as drawer/sheet
```

The message history remains accessible.

---

# 170. Chat Keyboard Behavior

Support:

- text input;
- Enter/send semantics;
- multiline composition;
- keyboard navigation;
- focus preservation.

Do not make Enter impossible to use for multiline text.

---

# 171. Chat Streaming Accessibility

Streaming responses should update without moving focus.

Completion may be announced once.

---

# 172. Chat Error Recovery

If an AI response fails:

- preserve the user's message;
- show failure;
- provide retry where supported;
- avoid silently deleting conversation context.

---

# 173. Responsive Help Requests

Help request forms should preserve:

- subject;
- description;
- priority;
- status;
- response history.

Mobile uses stacked sections.

---

# 174. Responsive Mentor Notes

Notes should remain readable and editable according to visibility/permission rules.

Long notes should not be trapped inside tiny cards.

---

# 175. Responsive Investigation Views

Admin investigation detail may use:

Desktop:

```text
case information | evidence/context
```

Mobile:

```text
case information
↓
evidence/context
```

Sensitive data remains permission-controlled.

---

# 176. Accessibility of Admin Interfaces

Admin density must not come at the cost of accessibility.

Dense information may use:

- compact spacing;
- structured tables;
- progressive disclosure.

Do not use tiny text as the primary density mechanism.

---

# 177. Accessible Dense Data

Dense data must retain:

- adequate line height;
- focus visibility;
- semantic relationships;
- readable labels.

---

# 178. Responsive Security Controls

Security/audit actions must remain accessible on mobile.

Critical actions must not disappear because a desktop toolbar cannot fit.

---

# 179. Accessibility of Destructive Actions

Destructive actions require:

- clear label;
- clear consequence;
- accessible confirmation;
- appropriate focus.

Avoid icon-only destructive buttons without labels.

---

# 180. Touch Confirmation

On touch devices, avoid destructive actions that can be triggered accidentally by a single tiny tap where confirmation is appropriate.

---

# 181. Accessible Loading Transitions

When content changes from loading to loaded:

- preserve meaningful focus;
- do not unexpectedly move users;
- expose completion only when meaningful.

---

# 182. Accessible Empty States

Empty states should expose:

```text
what is empty
why
next action
```

through semantic text.

---

# 183. Accessible Success States

Success feedback should remain available after a transient toast disappears.

The updated canonical state should communicate the result.

---

# 184. Accessible Error States

Error information must persist long enough to understand/recover.

Transient-only error messages are insufficient for critical operations.

---

# 185. Responsive Error Boundaries

Error boundaries must remain readable on small screens.

Recovery controls must remain accessible.

---

# 186. Responsive Offline State

Offline/degraded indicators should be:

- visible;
- compact;
- non-blocking when possible.

Critical mutation blocking must be clear.

---

# 187. Offline Accessibility

Offline status should be textual and not color-only.

---

# 188. Reconnection Accessibility

Reconnection should avoid repeatedly announcing:

```text
connecting
connecting
connecting
```

Use a concise status strategy.

---

# 189. Accessible Event Streams

Event-driven updates should have semantic UI representation.

Raw event payloads are not user-facing accessibility content.

---

# 190. Responsive Notification Flood Handling

Grouped notifications must remain individually understandable.

Do not create unreadable compressed notification summaries.

---

# 191. Responsive Activity Feed

Activity feeds may switch from dense table-like desktop rows to stacked event entries.

The semantic event description remains identical.

---

# 192. Responsive Documents List

Documents list may transform:

```text
Desktop → table
Tablet → reduced table
Mobile → structured list
```

Preserve version/status/action information.

---

# 193. Responsive GitHub View

GitHub information may move from multi-column summary to stacked cards/details.

External integration state remains clearly identified.

---

# 194. Responsive Risk List

Risks should prioritize:

```text
risk title
severity
status
mitigation/action
```

Additional metadata can move into detail.

---

# 195. Responsive Milestone List

Milestones should preserve:

- milestone identity;
- status;
- progress;
- timeline;
- task relationship.

---

# 196. Responsive Task Detail

Task detail should prioritize:

1. title;
2. status;
3. primary action;
4. milestone;
5. due date;
6. description;
7. supporting metadata.

---

# 197. Responsive Project Overview

Project overview should prioritize:

```text
project identity
lifecycle
health
progress
next action
critical risks
```

before secondary analytics.

---

# 198. Responsive Blueprint

Blueprint should prioritize:

```text
summary
architecture/structure
features
milestones/tasks
risks
technology
```

according to final product design.

---

# 199. Responsive Assessment

Assessment must remain focused.

Mobile:

```text
question
answers
progress
navigation
```

Avoid excessive surrounding chrome.

---

# 200. Assessment Accessibility

Questions and answers must support:

- keyboard;
- screen readers;
- clear selected state;
- error handling;
- progress communication.

---

# 201. Adaptive Assessment Accessibility

When the backend supplies a new question, focus should move predictably without causing disorientation.

---

# 202. Assessment Completion Accessibility

Completion should clearly communicate:

- assessment finished;
- result availability;
- next step.

---

# 203. Responsive Blueprint Generation

Generation UI should keep primary state visible:

```text
Generating
Current stage
Cancel if supported
```

Detailed agent information may be secondary.

---

# 204. Responsive Project Change Review

Current/proposed comparison must remain understandable on mobile.

Possible transformation:

```text
side-by-side desktop
→ stacked comparison mobile
```

---

# 205. Accessible Current/Proposed Comparison

Do not rely only on:

```text
red = removed
green = added
```

Use labels such as:

```text
Added
Removed
Changed
Unchanged
```

---

# 206. Responsive Document Generation

Generation status should remain visible while users navigate.

Completion should arrive through normal notification/event mechanisms.

---

# 207. Accessibility Definition of Responsive Success

A responsive feature is successful only when:

- information remains understandable;
- actions remain reachable;
- focus remains coherent;
- content does not unexpectedly overflow;
- keyboard/touch behavior works;
- assistive technology can understand state;
- security semantics remain unchanged.

---

# 208. Accessibility Definition of Done

A feature is accessibility-ready when:

- semantic HTML is used;
- keyboard access works;
- focus is visible;
- focus order is logical;
- dialogs are accessible;
- forms have labels;
- errors are associated;
- dynamic status is communicated appropriately;
- color is not the sole status cue;
- reduced motion is supported;
- zoom/reflow works;
- screen-reader behavior is tested.

---

# 209. Responsive Definition of Done

A feature is responsive-ready when:

- mobile composition is defined;
- tablet composition is defined;
- desktop composition is defined;
- overflow is intentional;
- touch targets are adequate;
- navigation remains accessible;
- primary actions remain discoverable;
- tables have a responsive strategy;
- forms remain usable;
- dashboards recompose;
- dialogs/drawers adapt;
- long content does not break layout.

---

# 210. Accessibility Testing Layers

Testing should include:

```text
Automated
 ↓
Component
 ↓
Keyboard
 ↓
Screen Reader
 ↓
Responsive
 ↓
Real-device/manual
```

Automated accessibility tests do not replace human evaluation.

---

# 211. Automated Accessibility

Use automated tooling to detect common problems:

- missing labels;
- invalid ARIA;
- contrast issues where detectable;
- landmark problems;
- heading issues;
- form associations.

---

# 212. Manual Keyboard Audit

Perform full keyboard walkthroughs of critical workflows.

Especially:

- login;
- project creation;
- assessment;
- blueprint generation;
- task management;
- document viewing;
- AI Mentor;
- help requests;
- mentor supervision;
- admin investigation.

---

# 213. Screen Reader Audit

Test representative end-to-end journeys, not only isolated components.

---

# 214. Real Device Testing

Test representative:

- iOS/mobile browser;
- Android/mobile browser;
- tablet;
- desktop.

Exact browser matrix may be established during implementation.

---

# 215. Network + Responsive Testing

Combine:

- small viewport;
- slow network;
- streaming;
- navigation;
- reconnect.

This catches failures that separate tests may miss.

---

# 216. Accessibility + Streaming Testing

Test:

```text
AI running
→ screen reader
→ navigation
→ reconnect
→ completion
```

Ensure no announcement flood or focus loss.

---

# 217. Accessibility + Error Testing

Test:

```text
form error
API error
403
404
network error
AI failure
```

with keyboard and assistive technology.

---

# 218. Responsive + Error Testing

Every major page should have responsive states for:

```text
loading
empty
error
success
streaming
```

---

# 219. Responsive + Permission Testing

Check permission-aware controls at all breakpoints.

Do not accidentally expose hidden desktop actions on mobile.

---

# 220. Accessibility + Security

Accessibility improvements must never weaken:

- authorization;
- privacy;
- secure file handling;
- sensitive content boundaries.

---

# 221. ARIA Policy

ARIA should supplement semantic HTML, not replace it unnecessarily.

Avoid:

```text
role="button"
```

on a `div` when a native button is possible.

---

# 222. ARIA State Accuracy

ARIA states must reflect actual UI state.

Examples:

```text
aria-expanded
aria-selected
aria-checked
aria-busy
aria-invalid
```

Never leave stale ARIA state after a transition.

---

# 223. Hidden Content

Content hidden visually may still be exposed to assistive technology unless intentionally controlled.

Use hiding mechanisms deliberately.

---

# 224. Focus Trap Safety

Focus traps must always provide an escape path.

Never create a modal that traps keyboard users indefinitely.

---

# 225. Focus Restoration Safety

If the trigger disappears after an operation, focus should move to a meaningful replacement rather than a nonexistent element.

---

# 226. Accessible Icons in Tables

Row action icons must have row-specific context where needed.

Avoid multiple indistinguishable:

```text
Edit
Edit
Edit
```

without accessible association to the row.

---

# 227. Accessible Bulk Actions

Bulk actions should announce selected count and affected scope.

---

# 228. Accessible Filters

Filter controls must expose current selections.

Active filter state should be understandable without visual chips alone.

---

# 229. Accessible Sort State

Current sort direction must be programmatically understandable.

---

# 230. Accessible Pagination State

Current page must be identifiable.

---

# 231. Accessible Breadcrumbs

Breadcrumb navigation should use semantic navigation structure and identify the current page.

---

# 232. Accessible Top Navigation

Global navigation must expose:

- navigation landmark;
- link names;
- workplace selector;
- account controls.

---

# 233. Accessible Sidebar

Sidebar navigation must:

- have a navigation landmark;
- expose active route;
- remain keyboard reachable;
- work when collapsed;
- work inside mobile drawer.

---

# 234. Collapsed Sidebar Accessibility

When sidebar is collapsed:

- icons require accessible names;
- tooltips cannot be the only accessible label;
- active state remains clear.

---

# 235. Responsive Sidebar State Persistence

If sidebar collapse is a user preference, it may persist.

Mobile drawer open state should normally remain transient.

---

# 236. Accessibility and User Preferences

The system should respect:

- reduced motion;
- browser zoom;
- text scaling;
- system theme.

Where user preferences are persisted, they remain UI preferences rather than domain state.

---

# 237. Accessibility Documentation

Each reusable component should document:

```text
Keyboard behavior
Focus behavior
ARIA behavior
Responsive behavior
Reduced-motion behavior
```

where applicable.

---

# 238. Responsive Documentation

Each complex component should document:

```text
Desktop
Tablet
Mobile
Overflow
Touch
```

behavior.

---

# 239. Design Review Checklist

Before approving a Stitch design, review:

- desktop;
- tablet;
- mobile;
- loading;
- empty;
- error;
- success;
- permission;
- streaming;
- reduced motion;
- keyboard;
- accessibility;
- long-content behavior.

---

# 240. Stitch Constraints

Stitch-generated designs must not:

- create tiny mobile text;
- depend on hover;
- create inaccessible icon-only actions;
- introduce arbitrary breakpoints;
- create desktop-only functionality;
- hide essential content on mobile;
- use motion that violates reduced-motion behavior;
- create fake progress;
- introduce role-specific themes.

---

# 241. Stitch Responsive Prompt Requirements

Every future page prompt should specify:

```text
Desktop layout
Tablet adaptation
Mobile adaptation
Primary action priority
Overflow behavior
Navigation behavior
Accessibility expectations
Loading/error/empty states
```

---

# 242. Stitch Accessibility Prompt Requirements

Every applicable prompt should specify:

- semantic hierarchy;
- keyboard-accessible controls;
- visible focus;
- accessible labels;
- non-color status;
- reduced-motion compatibility.

---

# 243. Visual Review vs Behavioral Review

A Stitch design must pass two separate reviews:

```text
Visual review
→ Does it look correct?

Behavioral review
→ Does it behave correctly?
```

Passing only visual review is insufficient.

---

# 244. Responsive Visual Review

Review:

```text
desktop
tablet
mobile
```

side-by-side when possible.

---

# 245. Accessibility Visual Review

Check:

- contrast;
- focus visibility;
- text size;
- touch target size;
- status clarity;
- hierarchy.

---

# 246. Responsive Regression

When a desktop design changes, verify tablet/mobile.

When mobile changes, verify desktop/tablet.

Responsive layouts are one system, not independent designs.

---

# 247. Accessibility Regression

When component markup or interaction changes, rerun relevant accessibility tests.

---

# 248. Design Token Compliance

Responsive adaptations must use Phase 06C tokens.

Do not introduce arbitrary:

- spacing;
- colors;
- radii;
- shadows;
- motion.

---

# 249. Component Compliance

Responsive behavior must use Phase 06D components wherever possible.

Page-specific variants require justification.

---

# 250. Shell Compliance

Responsive shell behavior must follow Phase 06E.

Do not create a second navigation system for a page.

---

# 251. Behavior Compliance

Responsive behavior must follow Phase 06F.

Navigation must not terminate backend jobs.

SSE must reconnect.

Canonical state must remain backend-owned.

---

# 252. Performance on Mobile

Mobile implementation should prioritize:

- reduced JavaScript execution;
- lazy loading;
- optimized images;
- controlled animation;
- efficient event handling;
- limited simultaneous requests.

---

# 253. Low-Power Devices

Avoid unnecessary:

- continuous animations;
- high-frequency polling;
- expensive chart updates;
- large synchronous computations.

---

# 254. Large Data on Mobile

Do not download massive datasets merely because the desktop table expects them.

Use:

- pagination;
- filtering;
- targeted queries;
- detail routes.

---

# 255. Mobile AI Resource Use

AI streaming UI should not re-render the entire page for each token/event.

Use incremental, bounded updates.

---

# 256. Accessibility Performance

Accessibility features must not be implemented in ways that create excessive DOM complexity or repeated announcements.

---

# 257. Responsive Asset Loading

Use appropriate image sizes for viewport/device where supported.

Do not load desktop-resolution editorial assets unnecessarily on mobile.

---

# 258. Responsive Typography Performance

Font loading should remain efficient.

Manrope remains the canonical typeface.

Fallback behavior must remain readable while fonts load.

---

# 259. Font Accessibility

Font choice must not prevent:

- zoom;
- text scaling;
- high contrast;
- assistive technology.

---

# 260. Focus and Font Loading

Font swaps should avoid major layout shifts that move focused controls unexpectedly.

---

# 261. Layout Stability

Reserve space for:

- images;
- async content;
- notifications;
- banners;
- charts.

Avoid unexpected layout shifts.

---

# 262. Responsive Layout Stability

A mobile layout should not jump dramatically when:

- browser chrome changes;
- keyboard opens;
- async content arrives;
- notifications appear.

---

# 263. Safe Dynamic Banners

Global network/system banners should not cover primary content.

Content must reflow or reserve appropriate space.

---

# 264. Accessibility of Banners

Banners must communicate:

- severity;
- message;
- action;
- dismissal where available.

---

# 265. Dismissible Notices

Dismissal must be accessible.

If a critical system state persists, dismissal should not remove necessary status information entirely.

---

# 266. Responsive Confirmation Dialogs

Confirmation content must remain fully readable on mobile.

Buttons should remain separated and obvious.

---

# 267. Accessible Confirmation Dialogs

Confirmation should state:

```text
what will happen
```

and provide:

```text
confirm
cancel
```

with clear focus.

---

# 268. Responsive Menus

Menus must remain within viewport bounds.

If necessary, reposition rather than clip.

---

# 269. Responsive Popovers

Popovers must avoid:

- viewport clipping;
- inaccessible off-screen content;
- overlap with focused elements.

---

# 270. Accessible Context Menus

Context menus should have keyboard/touch equivalents and should never be the only path to important actions.

---

# 271. Responsive Toolbars

Toolbars may collapse secondary actions into a menu.

Primary actions remain directly visible.

---

# 272. Accessibility of Toolbars

Toolbar groups should expose meaningful labels/structure where needed.

---

# 273. Responsive Project Actions

Project-level actions should preserve priority:

```text
primary
→ secondary
→ overflow
```

Do not hide the primary action in a generic overflow menu on mobile.

---

# 274. Responsive Task Actions

Task actions should preserve the most frequent action directly.

Rare/destructive actions may move into overflow.

---

# 275. Responsive Risk Actions

Risk mitigation/resolution actions must remain discoverable.

---

# 276. Responsive Mentor Actions

Mentor actions should preserve high-value supervision tasks:

- review;
- assign;
- note;
- respond;
- inspect.

---

# 277. Responsive Admin Actions

Governance actions must preserve explicit labels and confirmation.

---

# 278. Accessibility of Role Context

The current workplace/role context should be understandable to assistive technology.

Do not rely only on accent color or visual logo treatment.

---

# 279. Responsive Role Context

On mobile, role/workplace context may appear in the page header or drawer header when useful.

---

# 280. Accessibility of Page Titles

Each route should have a meaningful document title and visible page heading where appropriate.

---

# 281. Route Transition Loading

When navigation loads a new route:

- expose loading status appropriately;
- avoid trapping focus;
- preserve shell.

---

# 282. Route Transition Errors

If a route fails to load:

- preserve shell;
- show local error;
- provide recovery.

---

# 283. Accessibility of Route Errors

Route errors should move focus appropriately so users know the page changed into an error state.

---

# 284. Responsive Route Errors

Error layouts must remain usable at all breakpoints.

---

# 285. Accessibility Acceptance Threshold

No major page is complete if a keyboard-only user cannot complete its primary workflow.

---

# 286. Responsive Acceptance Threshold

No major page is complete if its primary workflow becomes unusable on mobile.

---

# 287. Combined Acceptance Threshold

A major page is complete only when:

```text
Desktop
+
Tablet
+
Mobile
+
Keyboard
+
Assistive Technology
+
Reduced Motion
+
Error/Loading/Empty
```

are coherent.

---

# 288. Cross-Phase Invariants

These invariants must remain true:

1. One unified GrowFlow design system.
2. No separate role themes.
3. Warm light default and charcoal dark counterpart.
4. Manrope remains canonical.
5. Responsive behavior preserves domain semantics.
6. Backend remains authoritative.
7. SSE remains delivery only.
8. AI progress remains truthful.
9. Security is never weakened for responsive convenience.
10. Accessibility is not an optional visual enhancement.
11. Mobile is a first-class composition.
12. Tablet is explicitly considered.
13. Desktop density remains intentional.
14. Hover is never required for essential functionality.
15. Touch targets remain usable.
16. Keyboard navigation remains complete.
17. Focus remains coherent.
18. Reduced motion is respected.
19. Color is never the only semantic channel.
20. Dynamic content does not steal focus.
21. Long-running jobs survive navigation.
22. Responsive transformations do not alter domain data.
23. Hidden content does not leak protected information.
24. Charts have accessible alternatives where needed.
25. Dense admin interfaces remain accessible.
26. Stitch must represent responsive/accessibility states.
